---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import { NMETA } from "../lib/nutrition";
import { SITE, abs } from "../lib/site";
import { lastmodOf } from "../lib/lastmod";
const path = "/methodology/";
const ld = { "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "WebPage", name: "How we count calories and nutrients", url: abs(path), dateModified: lastmodOf(path), publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url } };
---
<Prose title="How We Count: USDA FoodData Central, Servings and Daily Values | Gunther Heller" h1="How we count" path={path} jsonld={ld}
  description="Where Gunther Heller's calorie and nutrient numbers come from (USDA FoodData Central), how servings and % Daily Values are calculated, and which foods get a page."
  lead="The short version: USDA measures the food, we do the arithmetic, and every page links to the USDA record so you can check it.">
  <h2>The source</h2>
  <p>All numbers come from <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/" rel="nofollow noopener">USDA FoodData Central</a>, run by the Agricultural Research Service. We use three of its public-domain datasets: Foundation Foods (April 2025), SR Legacy (April 2018) and FNDDS 2021–2023, the database behind the What We Eat in America survey. The source box at the bottom of each food page names the dataset and the FDC ID.</p>

  <h2>Choosing one record per food</h2>
  <p>USDA often has several records for what people think of as one food. For each food we keep the record that matches what most people mean (for "cooked rice", plain white rice, cooked) and show its USDA description on the page.</p>

  <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Servings.</strong> USDA gives every value per 100 g and lists gram weights for household measures. A serving is the per-100 g value times the weight, divided by 100. We skip measures nobody uses at home (school-lunch containers, "linear inches").</li>
    <li><strong>Calories</strong> are USDA's energy value in kcal, rounded to the nearest whole calorie.</li>
    <li><strong>% Daily Value</strong> uses the FDA's Daily Values for a 2,000-calorie diet (21 CFR 101.9). "High in protein" on a page means 20% of the Daily Value or more per serving, and "good source" means 10–19%, the FDA's own thresholds.</li>
    <li><strong>Net carbs</strong> are total carbohydrate minus fiber. Keto answers assume a 20–50 g daily allowance.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparisons</strong> rank a food among the other foods in its category on this site by calories per 100 g.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Restaurant items</h2>
  <p>Most <a href="/restaurants/">restaurant items</a> are USDA records: either USDA bought the item at the chain and analysed it in a lab (SR Legacy, 2018 or earlier), or it modelled the item from its recipe (FNDDS 2021–2023). The page says which. For chains USDA does not cover (Arby's, Dunkin', Five Guys), we copy the chain's own nutrition guide as printed, name the guide and its date on every item, and keep an item only if its calories agree with its protein, carbohydrate and fat (4, 4 and 9 calories per gram) within 20%. Some guides list values per serving without a weight; those pages show that serving only, with no per-100 g figures.</p>

  <h2>When USDA has no number</h2>
  <p>We leave the line out. A nutrient USDA did not measure is not shown as zero, and a section with nothing in it is not shown at all.</p>

  <h2>Which foods get a page</h2>
  <p>We publish {NMETA.count.toLocaleString("en-US")} foods: the ones people actually find this site for in Google Search, plus restaurant menu items. We do not publish a page for every USDA record.</p>

  <h2>Dates</h2>
  <p>"Updated" on a page, and the date in our sitemap, is the day that food's numbers last changed in our data. Restyling a page does not change it.</p>

  <h2>Mistakes</h2>
  <p>Use the "Report a correction" link on any food page, or email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. We compare your source with the USDA record. If our page is wrong we fix it; if USDA's record looks wrong we tell USDA and add a note to the page.</p>
</Prose>
